WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.350
The policy plane isn't one box.

2
00:00:02.350 --> 00:00:05.750
It has three parts, which
products package differently.

3
00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:11.747
An on-premises DNS and DHCP layer hosts
the internal zones that replace AD DNS,

4
00:00:11.747 --> 00:00:16.713
serves recursion, and applies
response policy zones and DNSSEC.

5
00:00:16.713 --> 00:00:19.083
Grid members answer from local copies,

6
00:00:19.083 --> 00:00:23.974
so the program's position is that changes
converge in seconds, not milliseconds,

7
00:00:23.974 --> 00:00:29.162
and an emergency block must be verified on
the members serving the affected clients.

8
00:00:29.162 --> 00:00:32.336
A management plane,
increasingly a cloud service,

9
00:00:32.336 --> 00:00:35.663
holds the system of record
and pushes it everywhere.

10
00:00:35.663 --> 00:00:38.619
And local resolvers and
agents keep resolving

11
00:00:38.619 --> 00:00:41.575
from local state if
management is unreachable.

12
00:00:41.575 --> 00:00:43.836
It's overlay, not replacement:

13
00:00:43.836 --> 00:00:48.075
Route 53 and Azure Private
DNS still serve their zones.

14
00:00:48.075 --> 00:00:54.513
Infoblox is the book's example: the NIOS
grid, and Universal DDI as the cloud plane.

15
00:00:54.513 --> 00:00:59.861
Its FedRAMP-listed offering is Infoblox
Government Cloud, certified at Moderate;

16
00:00:59.861 --> 00:01:02.772
on-premises NIOS appliances
aren't a FedRAMP

17
00:01:02.772 --> 00:01:06.075
cloud service and sit inside
the agency's boundary.

18
00:01:06.075 --> 00:01:10.463
Chapter 12 evaluates other platforms
against the same requirements.

19
00:01:10.463 --> 00:01:15.700
The architecture is the same everywhere;
authorization and integrations change.

20
00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:19.727
A plane certified at Moderate, plus
the agency's own authorization,

21
00:01:19.727 --> 00:01:24.487
suffices for Moderate systems but doesn't
cover High: use a service authorized

22
00:01:24.487 --> 00:01:28.587
at the required level or keep the
plane inside the agency's boundary.

23
00:01:28.587 --> 00:01:30.776
Azure Government's zone names differ.

24
00:01:30.776 --> 00:01:34.600
VPC IPAM's external
authority isn't in GovCloud.

25
00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:40.276
The Private Resolver is at FedRAMP
High in Azure Government and forecast,

26
00:01:40.276 --> 00:01:42.788
not yet authorized, at IL4 and IL5.

27
00:01:42.788 --> 00:01:46.287
Azure Private DNS can't
be signed anywhere.

28
00:01:46.287 --> 00:01:52.580
And informed network routing isn't
available in GCC-Moderate, GCC High or DoD.
